1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now, the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The other night, there was a horrible, horrible accident just before a scout meeting.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
One of the scouts ran into the meeting and said,

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
I'm afraid it looks like the scout master has been hit by a bus.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
And the troop committee chairman said, yeah, yeah it does,

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
but he's got a great personality and he's real good with the scouts.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Think about that one for a little bit.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Hey, this is podcast number 77.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Welcome back to the scout master podcast.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,000
This is Clark Green.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Okay, let's see what we've had come over the transom and in the mail bag

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
and on the emails and all that kind of stuff in the past week or so.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Walter Underwood was in touch with us about our last podcast.

14
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
He said, good advice on homesickness.

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
The buddy system helps too.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Their tent made as their buddy and we strongly encourage the scouts

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
to go around camp together.

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
It's easier to stay busy in this new strange place

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
and makes it less foreign when you have a buddy to enjoy it with.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Our camp director requests that adults keep cell phones

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
out of the campsites at all times.

22
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
If we do need to make a call, we can do that from the scout master's lounge

23
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
or from an isolated bench with good reception and a great view.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Sometimes just seeing a cell phone makes new scouts think about calling home

25
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and I have had them beg me to make a call when they were fine

26
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
just before they saw the phone.

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
But luckily our campsite doesn't have any signal.

28
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
So thanks, Walter.

29
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,000
You were not alone in your observations about calling home.

30
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Joe said this.

31
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
He said, I agree with most of what you're saying about coping with homesickness

32
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
except the calling a home part.

33
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
I couldn't disagree more after numerous years of summer camp,

34
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
calling home has always proven to be counterproductive.

35
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Well, I'll tell you something.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
I may have not made myself clear in the last podcast

37
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
when we talked about calling home as being a cure for homesickness.

38
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
We don't call home in the middle of like a bout of homesickness

39
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
where the boy is going crazy and everything like that.

40
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
What I use that particular measure for is as a touchstone

41
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
as some kind of a way for a boy to realize that things are fine back home.

42
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Everybody's good.

43
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
There's no big problems.

44
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
And most of the time, actually all of the time this works fine.

45
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Because I speak to the parents before the boy speaks to the parents

46
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
and I explain the situation to them out of earshot of the boy.

47
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,000
I say, hey, you know, I've got John over here.

48
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
He's been feeling a little homesick.

49
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
I promised him that he could call home.

50
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
And I would just want us to both be on the same page.

51
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
I don't want to have any problems about us disagreeing

52
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
as to whether he should stay for the rest of the week or not.

53
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
I know that that's a commitment that we've all made around making this happen.

54
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
I just think it might do him some good if he can speak with you

55
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
and realize that that's not an option.

56
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
And then, you know, sometimes that conversation goes on

57
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
for a little while explaining to parents, hey, you know,

58
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
it's not going to be all that bad for us here

59
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
because a lot of times they're very apologetic about,

60
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
oh, I'm so sorry you're going through this and everything.

61
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Hey, no big deal, broad shoulders, big group.

62
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
We can take it, not a problem.

63
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
And, you know, that's the way we, and like I say,

64
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
maybe I didn't explain it very well in the last podcast,

65
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:10,000
but that's the way I use phone calls as far as something to mitigate

66
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
or to work with home sick scouts.

67
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Bill McFarland wrote in and said,

68
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
years ago at CubCamp, some of my scouts were a bit nervous

69
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
about being away from home.

70
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Part of it was being in the dark in a strange place

71
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
by simply hanging one of those light sticks,

72
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
one of those chemical lights in a tent.

73
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
It relieved their concerns.

74
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Now as the scout master, I'm pleased to see those same scouts

75
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
as youth leaders using the same trick with first-year campers.

76
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
And that's actually, you know, that's a pretty smart idea.

77
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
I've seen some flashlight batteries be very short-lived

78
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,000
because some of the boys want to keep those flashlights on all night.

79
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
And if they need something in the way of a little security blanket,

80
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
maybe one of those light sticks is a good idea.

81
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
As a matter of fact, I might throw a few of them in the kit

82
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
when I'm headed off to camp here in a week.

83
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
So just to sew a button on all that, you know,

84
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
in 27 years of summer camp,

85
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
only lost one boy to homesickness.

86
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And that was because he kind of hornswaggled

87
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
the headquarter staff into letting him use the telephone.

88
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
He called home, and he said,

89
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
come and pick me up.

90
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
So that's the only time that's ever happened.

91
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Moving along in this week's podcast

92
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
in Scout Mastership in 7 Minutes or Less,

93
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
we're going to talk a little bit about

94
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
adults attending scout camping trips.

95
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
This was a very popular post that was authored by

96
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
my friend and contributor, Larry Geiger,

97
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
who's a scoutmaster down there in Florida.

98
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
And I think it got so much response.

99
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
And I think it's such a bunch of good ideas

100
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
that I want to go ahead and feature it

101
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
over these next two podcasts.

102
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
And he's got 10 different tips

103
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
for working with adults attending scout camping trips.

104
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
So we'll do five this time and five next time.

105
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
And after we've done that,

106
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
we'll talk a little bit about some comments

107
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
that went back and forth on the blog this past week

108
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
over an old post about Merit Badge Counseling.

109
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
So I think that'll be enough for this podcast.

110
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Hey, let's get started, shall we?

111
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Scout Mastership in 7 Minutes or Less?

112
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:41,000
So this, as promised, is the first of a two-part talk

113
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
about parents coming on scout camping trips.

114
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
What we want to do when we bring parents on the trips

115
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
is we want them to be able to do the right stuff.

116
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Larry says that every once in a while he walks into camp,

117
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
he finds a parent sitting in their son's tent

118
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
organizing their son's gear

119
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
and maybe taking care of their tentmate's gear too

120
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
because it is so messy.

121
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Or over in his son's patrol campsite,

122
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
getting involved in making scrambled eggs

123
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
or grilling cheese sandwiches or something like that.

124
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,000
And that, if you're a scout master,

125
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
you know what happens when you see something like that.

126
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
It kind of sets the alarm bells off in your head

127
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
because scout camping is about scouts and patrols

128
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
operating independent of adult interference or help.

129
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
And this is so important

130
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
that we have to make sure that any adults

131
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
who are going along camping with us get it.

132
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,000
They need to get that fact.

133
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Now, as I said, there's different ways of looking at this,

134
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
but let's look at this from the standpoint

135
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
of somebody who encourages as many adults

136
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
to come on campouts and to summer camp as possible.

137
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
And it's a great opportunity

138
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
for a little informal adult leader training.

139
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
And, you know, especially summer camp,

140
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
that we could summer camp.

141
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
We can campouts.

142
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
It can be a little busy and, you know,

143
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
it might be hard to take a fair chunk of time

144
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
away from the scouts to be able to work

145
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
with the adults that are along.

146
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
We try to be very specific with instructions

147
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
for new adults.

148
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
They need to stay in the adult leader area.

149
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And that's an important piece.

150
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
And if you're not there yet,

151
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
if that's not what you're doing as a troop

152
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
when you go camping,

153
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
that's one thing that you need to pick up on

154
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
is there's a place for the adults to stay

155
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
and there's a place for the scouts to stay.

156
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
I know that I've had many parents come along

157
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
on camping trips where they just assume,

158
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
well, you know, I'll share this tent with my son.

159
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
And, you know, it'll be this nice kind of father-son,

160
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
mother-son time that we're going to have and everything.

161
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
And I have to get right in there

162
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and make sure that that is never an assumption

163
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
from the get-go.

164
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
If I know a parent's going to accompany us

165
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
on a camping trip,

166
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
I make sure that they understand that scouts

167
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
share tents with scouts, adults share tents with adults,

168
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
and we have our own place

169
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
and a way that we do things when we're camping.

170
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Most parents that go on camping trips,

171
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
they pick up on, you know, the whole deal.

172
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:24,000
They'll watch you and your other more experienced leaders

173
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
and take their clues from them.

174
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
And some of them just don't see it

175
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
and they kind of walk all over your normal expectations.

176
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
And so you have to get very specific with them.

177
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
And so number one is,

178
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
and is getting specific with parents and saying this,

179
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
unless there's an emergency,

180
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
adult leaders stay out of patrol camping areas.

181
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
I'll often send a patrol leader

182
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
or a senior patrol leader over there,

183
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
but we adults generally stay away.

184
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
And I hope you don't get tired of this particular illustration,

185
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
but, you know, think of it as the wild animal park.

186
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
You get to stay in the car,

187
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
watch the wild animals, the patrol over there,

188
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
but you don't get out of the car.

189
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
You know, if they come up to the car, hey, that's great,

190
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
but you don't get out of the car

191
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
and you let them do their own thing.

192
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Number two is we stay out of scouts' tents.

193
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:23,000
We just stay out of them, including your own boys' tent.

194
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,000
I wouldn't even think about going into a scouts' tent

195
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,000
unless there was an issue that requires my intervention

196
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,000
and I'm asked specifically to intervene,

197
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
and this almost never happens.

198
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
I mean, the main exception to this is a scout who's sick

199
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,000
and really does need some adult assistance.

200
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
Other than that, I stay out of their tents.

201
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
I leave them be.

202
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Number three, as I said before,

203
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,000
quickly and efficiently establish the adult area

204
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
at any camp as soon as possible

205
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
because the adults need a place to go.

206
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
They need to set up their chairs

207
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
and their cooking area and their tents and things like that,

208
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
and we need to tell any new leaders

209
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,000
or new parents that are out on a trip

210
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
where they may put their tents.

211
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
And if you do this right,

212
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
you'll have the senior patrol leader do it.

213
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:23,000
But it might not always work for various reasons,

214
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
and the scout master has to be able to back them up.

215
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
The adult area, if it's possible,

216
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
should be out of direct sight of the patrol areas, too,

217
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:39,000
because there's no reason for adults to sit there

218
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and keep an eagle eye on what's happening in a patrol area.

219
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
Number four, if you have a troop trailer,

220
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
don't let adults inside it.

221
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
I mean, I'm not a big guy for a troop trailer.

222
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
We've tried it, didn't like it very much.

223
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
Let the boys get the gear that they need.

224
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Let them take care of it. Keep adults out of it.

225
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:07,000
And I think that's a pretty wise bit of advice from Larry there.

226
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
If they need to do some construction work on the trailer

227
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
or some organizing or something like that,

228
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
let them do it at home.

229
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Otherwise, they're just going to be getting in the way

230
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
of the quartermaster.

231
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
And number five, think about setting up two campfires,

232
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
one for adults and one for scouts.

233
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,000
Now, if that's not always possible, that's okay.

234
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
But set up the two campfires.

235
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Let the scouts invite you to join them in the evening.

236
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
If you're in a campsite with only one fire ring,

237
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
then the scouts get to be close in

238
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and the adults sit together in the back.

239
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Some of the best memories you have as a kid,

240
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:48,000
being a scout,

241
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
are being able to sit around the campfire at night

242
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
with your buddies and swap stories and just kind of be.

243
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
And I think that's a good thing for them

244
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
to be able to do on their own.

245
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:01,000
So that's the first five of these ten tips

246
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:05,000
for bringing adults along on scout camping trips.

247
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
And we'll hit the other five in the next edition

248
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

249
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:13,000
Write me a letter, send it by name.

250
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Email that is, folks.

251
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

252
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
Last week over on the blog,

253
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:35,000
I had a back and forth conversation through comments

254
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
with somebody about a post on advancement.

255
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:45,000
And I'm going to keep the commenter's identity anonymous

256
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:46,000
in this case,

257
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
because I don't think he was very happy with me.

258
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,000
And I kind of, I wasn't really happy with him either.

259
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,000
So, and we got into a little bit of an argument.

260
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:02,000
But I think the subject matter is always relevant.

261
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
And I think it's important to make sure

262
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
that we're kind of all on the same page

263
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
about this sort of thing.

264
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:15,000
So, the back and forth all began with this comment

265
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,000
that he wrote.

266
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
He said, you seem to think that the Maripatch counselor

267
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,000
has the final word on a scout receiving a Maripatch.

268
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
I ran into a problem with this once

269
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
when a Maripatch counselor signed off a camping Maripatch

270
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:32,000
and I knew that the scout had not completed a requirement.

271
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Prior to my getting this blue card,

272
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
the scout had told me he was going to lie

273
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
about how he had fulfilled the requirement.

274
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
I told the scout and the counselor

275
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
that I would send in the advancement form

276
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
when the requirement had been met.

277
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
A couple of weeks later,

278
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,000
I received another signed blue card for the same badge

279
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:56,000
and the requirement had still not been met.

280
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
The counselor told me he had talked the scout

281
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
and felt like he deserved the badge.

282
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,000
I reiterated I would not approve a Maripatch

283
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
that I knew wasn't completed.

284
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
Finally, the scout went on a camping experience

285
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,000
to fulfill the requirement

286
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
and I sent in the advancement form.

287
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
I don't feel that I overstepped any boundary in doing this.

288
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
I feel like it would have been out of line

289
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
if I allowed the scout to receive something

290
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
that we all knew he had not earned,

291
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
especially a Maripatch so close to the core

292
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
of the scouting experience.

293
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
So that was the first comment.

294
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Now, this story is not all that uncommon.

295
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
If we're in the position of scout master

296
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and we suspect or we have direct knowledge

297
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:43,000
of what seems to be something not right

298
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:47,000
about the way a Maripatch is being counseled

299
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:51,000
or whether or not we agree

300
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
that a scout has fulfilled the requirements

301
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
for a Maripatch, what are we supposed to do?

302
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
Well, the story that I got through this comment

303
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
is that the scout master decided

304
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:08,000
that he would not submit the advancement form.

305
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
And so there's alarm number one

306
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
because there's something wrong there.

307
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
So let's just kind of take this apart

308
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:20,000
and look at policy statements and procedures

309
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
that affect this type of thing.

310
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Let's assume that we know

311
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:29,000
that there's some kind of a problem with a Maripatch.

312
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,000
We have direct positive knowledge

313
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
that a scout didn't do something

314
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
yet is still bringing you a blue card

315
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,000
that has been signed by the Maripatch counselor.

316
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:46,000
And you then have to take action.

317
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
So what do you do?

318
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
What do you do?

319
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
Well, before I answer that question,

320
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
let's take a look at a blue card.

321
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,000
You sign, as a unit leader, you sign a blue card twice.

322
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
The first time you sign it

323
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:06,000
is to certify that a scout

324
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
is a member in good standing of his unit

325
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
and qualifies, therefore, to go ahead

326
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
and take a Maripatch.

327
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:17,000
That's simple, okay?

328
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:21,000
It has nothing to do with anything other than the fact

329
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,000
that he is signed up and part of your unit.

330
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
It's not that you particularly approve of him

331
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:28,000
taking the Maripatch,

332
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
that you particularly approve of the counselor

333
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:31,000
or anything like that.

334
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
This is, oh, yeah, he's signed up and everything.

335
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Now, you've probably asked him

336
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
if the counselor that he is going to use

337
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
is a registered Maripatch counselor,

338
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
and you've reviewed with him the idea

339
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
that he goes to visit the counselor or the buddy

340
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:47,000
and all that type of thing.

341
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
Yes, okay.

342
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
But that signature, that first signature,

343
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,000
is simply a certification that he is currently

344
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
registered with your troop

345
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and therefore qualifies to go and take a Maripatch

346
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
because any scout can challenge any Maripatch

347
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
at any time.

348
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Secondly, the second time you're going to sign off

349
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,000
on the Maripatch blue card

350
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
is when the scout brings it back

351
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and he's completed the Maripatch application,

352
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:20,000
that's the official name of what we commonly call

353
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:21,000
the blue card.

354
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
He hands it to you and as a record

355
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
that he has submitted it,

356
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
he gets the one panel of the blue card

357
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:33,000
that says for the applicant or for the scout

358
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,000
and you sign that just to certify that

359
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,000
he has given you a completed Maripatch application.

360
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
That's all.

361
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
Not that you approve of it,

362
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
not that you think that he completed it,

363
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
nothing like that at all.

364
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Just that you have received it.

365
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:52,000
The scout master signing the blue card

366
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
has absolutely nothing to do

367
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
with the Maripatch being approved.

368
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
Has nothing to do with quality control.

369
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,000
Has nothing to do with the integrity of the program.

370
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
What it has to do with is

371
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:07,000
a couple of very simple administrative facts.

372
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,000
If we go to the Advancement Committee guidebook

373
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and we look on page 17,

374
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:13,000
it says this,

375
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
the responsibility for Maripatches

376
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
shall rest with the Maripatch counselor

377
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
approved by the local council

378
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
and district advancement committee.

379
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
Maripatch counselors shall be registered

380
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
adult members of the Boy Scouts of America.

381
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
The Maripatch counselor shall prepare

382
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
and qualify youth members.

383
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
There shall be no Board of Review procedure

384
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
for Maripatches.

385
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,000
And then a few pages later on page 26

386
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
in the Advancement Committee guide it says,

387
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
a Maripatch cannot be taken away

388
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
once it has been earned provided

389
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
the counselor is a registered counselor

390
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
for the Maripatch.

391
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
The Maripatch counselor is

392
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
the final word on a Maripatch.

393
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
It's not that I think that he is.

394
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
It's not that I feel that he is

395
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
or anything like that.

396
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
It's just very, very plain.

397
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
There's also a statement on the blue card

398
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
that says that.

399
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
The Maripatch counselor has the final word

400
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
on the Maripatch.

401
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
If the Maripatch counselor signs the blue card,

402
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
the Maripatch is done, the scout has earned the badge.

403
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
Now,

404
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
part of the back and forth

405
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
with this particular commenter on the blog

406
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
was he was trying to parse the term

407
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
earned the badge.

408
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
He says, well, obviously he hasn't earned the badge

409
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
because I know it.

410
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
There's no condition of authority

411
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
to be able to decide that.

412
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
The only person who can decide

413
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
whether a scout has earned a badge or not

414
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
is a Maripatch counselor.

415
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
Now, before

416
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
before you go off the deep end here, folks,

417
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
that's a great

418
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
procedure and a great rule.

419
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
There's no reviewing.

420
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
There's no recourse.

421
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
There's nothing that happens.

422
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Now, Clark, come on.

423
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
Even if I absolutely know

424
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
that the Maripatch counselor has

425
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
earned the badge

426
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
and he brings me a blue card

427
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
signed by a Maripatch counselor,

428
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
I still have to sign it.

429
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
I still have to submit it

430
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
to the advancement committee,

431
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
then submit the advancement report, etc.

432
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
I have to shake the boys' hand

433
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
and I have to give them the Maripatch.

434
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
Yes, that is true

435
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
because you are not always right

436
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
and a Maripatch counselor isn't always right

437
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
and a scout isn't always right.

438
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
If you don't have direct knowledge

439
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
that this is not right,

440
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
I understand that.

441
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
That's not the issue.

442
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
The issue is one of authority

443
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
and the issue is one of policy

444
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
and procedure.

445
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
Now, if scoutmasters

446
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
were the final arbiters

447
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
of whether a Maripatch

448
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
had been completed or not,

449
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
if scoutmasters were the final judge

450
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
of vetting

451
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
a Maripatch counselor

452
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
and making sure they were suitable

453
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
and everything like that,

454
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
it would be a different story, but we're not.

455
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
And really, thank goodness we're not

456
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
because then

457
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
we would have to examine

458
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
each and every Maripatch.

459
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
We would have to examine the requirements

460
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
that each scout did.

461
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
We would have to examine each counselor.

462
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
There's not enough time in the day

463
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
for us to do that

464
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
and it's just not proper for us to do that.

465
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
We sit up and we say

466
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
trustworthy.

467
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
We stand up and we hold up the scout sign

468
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
and we say that at least once a week

469
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
most of us, right?

470
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
A scout is trustworthy.

471
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
If a scout is trustworthy

472
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
then when he brings you a blue card

473
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
that blue card is

474
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
his word.

475
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
It's also the Maripatch counselor's word

476
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
and we don't second guess it.

477
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
We just don't.

478
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
Now, 27 years,

479
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
lots and lots of Maripatches

480
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
have ever figured out how many.

481
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
Thousands by now.

482
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
Signed lots and lots and lots of blue cards.

483
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
Also saw it in summer camp

484
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
as a camp counselor

485
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
and as a camp director.

486
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
And

487
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
there will never be a way

488
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
for you to be satisfied

489
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
100%

490
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
with every single thing that has ever done for a Maripatch.

491
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
But let's

492
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
assume

493
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
that as in the commenter

494
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
we started out with here

495
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
that you have absolute direct knowledge

496
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
that something is rotten in Denmark

497
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
and this isn't working.

498
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
Do you still have to sign the blue card? Yes.

499
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
I think we answered that question.

500
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
Yes, you do.

501
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
You still have to go through the whole thing

502
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
and because you're a big guy, you're going to shake the scout's hand

503
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
and you're going to hand him the Maripatch

504
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
because it's not the scout's fault.

505
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
It's not the scout's fault.

506
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
It

507
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
has got to have something to do

508
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
with the Maripatch Counselor now.

509
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
So how do we

510
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
fix this?

511
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
Do we sit down

512
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
and fire off a nasty email

513
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
to the Maripatch Counselor? No.

514
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:55,000
Do we get them on the phone and dress them down

515
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,000
and no.

516
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
Do we go to them in person

517
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,000
and wag our finger at them and haunt them? No.

518
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,000
Who certifies Maripatch Counselors?

519
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:05,000
That's right.

520
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
The District

521
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,000
and the Council Advancement Committee

522
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
each one of those committees has a chairman

523
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
and you know what their job is?

524
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
Their job is to make sure that things are going smooth

525
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
and that Maripatch Counselors are doing their job

526
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
and that

527
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:21,000
scouts are earning Maripatches.

528
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
That's their job, not your job.

529
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:25,000
So if you know, if you have direct knowledge

530
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
of something being done

531
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
improperly, who do you go to?

532
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
That's right. You go to that committee chairman

533
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:33,000
and you let them know

534
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
and then you drop it.

535
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
You let them take whatever action they're going to take.

536
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,000
They may have questions for you later on

537
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
and then you just drop it

538
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
and leave it be.

539
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,000
The way that this story was put to me,

540
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
I have no doubt that this particular

541
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
scout master felt like

542
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
he was trying to do the right thing.

543
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
He was trying to protect the advancement program.

544
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
He was trying to protect the integrity

545
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
of scouting and everything

546
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
but it's just simply not our job.

547
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
Not in that particular way.

548
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
We have other things to do.

549
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
We have other things to work at.

550
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
We have an advancement system

551
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
that if we

552
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
administer it properly

553
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
will take care of itself.

554
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:15,000
So

555
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,000
those are some thoughts and

556
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:19,000
some little facts

557
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:21,000
and pieces of policy

558
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,000
that affect the way Maripatches work.

559
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
Now I'm very interested to hear what you think.

560
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,000
I'm very interested to be told

561
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:29,000
that I'm absolutely wrong

562
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
but I want you to back it up

563
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
not with what you feel

564
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,000
not with the way that

565
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,000
you think things maybe should go

566
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
but with policy.

567
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
Show me in black and white

568
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
in BSA policy that I'm wrong

569
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
and boy I'll tell you

570
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
I'm happy to learn.

571
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:49,000
I would like to learn

572
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,000
because I learned

573
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,000
this through

574
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,000
my own hard experience

575
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:57,000
with suspecting Maripatch counselors

576
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
and not wanting to sign blue cards

577
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
and trying to insert myself

578
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
into a process that I didn't belong in.

579
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
Somebody

580
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
had to show me policy

581
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
and had to show me

582
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
what this really meant.

583
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,000
So

584
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,000
if I'm wrong, let me know

585
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,000
and let me know your ideas, let me know your experiences

586
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
around this because it's a pretty common problem.

587
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
It's a pretty common problem

588
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
and sometimes we try and do things

589
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
and we've got it

590
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
we're a little off course

591
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,000
so we need to get together

592
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:31,000
with folks and we need to try and figure out

593
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
the best way to make it work.

594
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:47,000
Thanks for listening to

595
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
this edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

596
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
Now, it is summertime

597
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:53,000
I am preparing

598
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
to be away for a few weeks

599
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
doing some scouting and some traveling

600
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
and I might not

601
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
be getting back to everybody as quickly

602
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:03,000
as I would like when you get in touch.

603
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:05,000
I do have a bunch of blog posts

604
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:07,000
and podcasts lined up

605
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:09,000
to automatically appear

606
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
when I am absent

607
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
